hadiths[94]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6984","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, apportioned roles among the communities of the Muslims. > **""Caliphate shall remain in Quraysh, judgment shall remain in the Ansar, the call shall remain in Abyssinia, and emigration shall remain among the Muslims, and emigrants shall continue thereafter.""**"
"6985","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, described where these trusts would endure. > **""Sovereignty shall remain in Quraysh, the judiciary in the Ansar, the call to prayer in Abyssinia, fidelity to covenant in Yemen, and trustworthiness in Azd.""**"
"6986","ʿImran ibn Hittan visited ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and they discussed judges, whereupon she reported what she heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""There shall surely come upon the just judge on the Day of Resurrection an hour in which he will wish that he had never judged between two persons, even over a single date.""**"
"6987","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning the fate of every leader. > **""There is no commander of ten except that he shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection bound in chains; nothing shall release him except justice.""**"
"6988","ʿAbdullah ibn Mawhab reports that ʿUthman, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, to judge among the people. Ibn ʿUmar asked to be excused, sought refuge in Allah from the office, and cited what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""Whoever seeks refuge in Allah has sought refuge in a mighty refuge. Whoever acts as judge and judges in ignorance shall be among the people of the Fire. Whoever acts as judge, possessing knowledge, and judges with truth or justice, shall beg to depart from it with neither gain nor loss.""**"
"6989","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, refused the judgeship offered by ʿUthman, may Allah be pleased with him, citing the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""Judges are three: one saved and two in the Fire. Whoever judges with injustice or desire shall perish, and whoever judges with truth shall be saved. A judge who judges by desire is in the Fire, a judge who judges without knowledge is in the Fire, and a judge who judges with truth is in Paradise.""**"
"6990","Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, promised divine aid to the just arbiter. > **""The Hand of Allah is with the judge when he judges, and the Hand of Allah is with the divider when he divides.""**"
"6991","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, raises this warning to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""The judge shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection and halted at the brink of Hell. If it is commanded concerning him and he is thrust in, he shall fall therein for seventy years.""**"
"6992","Maʿqil ibn Yasar al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded him to judge between a people. When he expressed his inadequacy, the Prophet reassured him with a condition. > **""The Hand of Allah is with the judge so long as he does not deliberately commit injustice.""**"
"6993","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""Allah is with the judge so long as he does not deliberately commit injustice.""**"
"6994","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, distinguished between the sincere ruler and the insincere. > **""Whoever assumes charge of any affair of the Muslims with the intention of truth, Allah appoints over him two angels who grant him success and guide him. Whoever assumes charge of any affair of the Muslims with an intention other than truth, Allah abandons him to himself.""**"
"6995","Wathilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, described the angels appointed over every Muslim authority. > **""There is no Muslim who assumes charge of any affair of the Muslims except that Allah sends to him two angels who keep him upright so long as he intends truth. When he deliberately intends injustice, they abandon him to himself.""**"
"6996","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""Allah, mighty and majestic, is with the judge so long as he does not deliberately commit injustice; He keeps him upright toward good so long as he does not intend otherwise.""**"
"6997","ʿImran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warned every Muslim judge. > **""There is no judge among the judges of the Muslims except that with him are two angels who keep him upright toward truth so long as he does not intend otherwise. When he intends otherwise and deliberately wrongs, the two angels disavow him and abandon him to himself.""**"
"6998","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade judgment in anger. > **""Whoever is tried with judging between the Muslims, let him not judge while he is angry.""**"
"6999","ʿUrwah ibn Muhammad ibn ʿAtiyyah reports from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with them, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warned against the anger of rulers. > **""When the ruler blazes with rage, Satan gains dominion.""**"
"7000","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, required composure for judgment. > **""The judge shall not judge between two persons except while he is satiated and quenched.""**"
"7001","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that two litigants disputed before ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs, may Allah be pleased with him. The one against whom judgment was given complained to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who explained the reward of judicial striving. > **""When the judge judges, striving, and is correct, he shall have ten rewards. When he strives and errs, he shall have one reward""** — or he said — **""two rewards.""**"
"7002","ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAs, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that two litigants came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who commanded ʿAmr to judge between them. ʿAmr deferred to the Prophet, who insisted, and then asked what reward would be his. > **""If you judge between them and judge correctly, you shall have ten good deeds. If you strive and err, you shall have one good deed.""**"
"7003","ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, a like account concerning the striving judge. > **""If you strive and are correct, you shall have ten rewards. If you strive and err, you shall have one reward.""**"
"7004","ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmir al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, while two litigants disputed before him. The Prophet commanded him to judge, and when he asked upon what basis, the Prophet instructed him to strive. > **""Strive. If you are correct, you shall have ten good deeds. If you are not correct, you shall have one good deed.""**"
"7005","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning the three judges. > **""Judges are three: a man who judges, striving, and is correct — for him is Paradise; a man who judges, striving, and errs — for him is Paradise; and a man who judges with injustice — he is in the Fire.""**"
"7006","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn Jawshan reports that Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to his son when he was governor of Sijistan, conveying what he heard from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. > **""Let no one give two conflicting judgments in a single matter.""**"
"7007","ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that a dispute arose between her and the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, whereupon he offered to appoint an arbiter, first ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, whom she declined, then her father Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, whom she accepted. > **""Shall I place ʿUmar between me and you?""** — she said: **""No.""** — he said: **""Shall I place your father between me and you?""** — she said: **""Yes.""**"
"7008","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that neither the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, nor Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, nor ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, appointed a standing judge, until at the end of his caliphate ʿUmar asked Yazid, the nephew of Yaman, to relieve him of some minor matters."
"7009","Al-Saʾib ibn Yazid, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, took no judge, and that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, was the first to appoint one, saying: keep the people away from me concerning cases of one or two dirhams."
"7010","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, appointed Ibn Umm Maktum, may Allah be pleased with him, a blind Companion, as his deputy over the prayer and other affairs of Madinah in his absence."
"7011","ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, linked divine purification to justice for the weak. > **""Allah does not purify a nation in which the right of the weak is not taken from the strong.""**"
"7012","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, made unhindered justice the mark of a purified nation. > **""No nation is purified in which the weak is not given his right without obstruction.""**"
"7013","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, arrived in Madinah and allotted dwellings, granting him a share among others, some Companions objected to his proximity. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, rebuked them. > **""Then for what did Allah send me? Allah does not purify a nation whose people do not give the weak among them his right.""**"
"7014","Qabus ibn Mukhariq reports from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warned against neglecting the weak. > **""No nation is purified in which the right of the weak is not secured for him without obstruction.""**"
"7015","Masruq reports that ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, disapproved of the judge of the Muslims taking a stipend for his judging, and likewise the one in charge of their spoils taking pay."
"7016","ʿAli, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, forbade rebuking one of the two disputants to the exclusion of the other, commanding equality between them."
"7017","Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, commanded complete impartiality of the judge. > **""When one of you is tried with judging between the Muslims, let him not judge while angry. Let him treat them equally in gaze, seating, and gesture, and let him not raise his voice against one of the two litigants above the other.""**"
"7018","Abu Musa al-Ashʿari, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that Muʿawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with them both, asked him whether the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, when two men disputed before him and agreed to return at an appointed time, would judge for the one who came against the one who failed to come. Abu Musa replied that such a ruling concerned beasts, sheep, and camels, whereas their present matter concerned the governance of the people."
"7019","ʿImran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warned the one who evades a lawful summons. > **""Whoever is summoned before a judge among the judges of the Muslims and does not come to him is unjust""** — or he said — **""he has no right.""**"
"7020","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, would say concerning two disputants when one calls the other to a judge and the other refuses. > **""When a man claims against another, and one of them calls his fellow to the one who will judge between them, and he refuses to come, then he has no right.""**"
"7021","Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, addressed them concerning disputants when one invites the other to the Messenger to judge between them. > **""When a man disputes with another, and one of them calls his fellow to the Messenger to judge between them, whoever refuses to come has no right.""**"
"7022","On the authority of Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said concerning the one summoned by authority. > **""Whoever is summoned before the authority and does not respond is unjust; he has no right.""**"
"7023","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that two men disputed before the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, who reminded them of the limits of human judgment. > **""I am only a human. I judge between you by what I hear from you. Perhaps one of you is more eloquent in his plea than his brother. Whomever I award anything of his brother's right, I have only cut for him a piece of the Fire.""**"
"7024","Thawban, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cursed corruption in judgment, including the intermediary who walks between the two parties. > **""The Messenger of Allah cursed the briber, the bribed, and the go-between""** — meaning the one who walks between them."
"7025","ʿAʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, cursed both sides of bribery. > **""The Messenger of Allah cursed the briber and the bribed.""**"
"7026","ʿAbd al-Rahman ibn ʿAwf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warned of the end of bribery. > **""The briber and the bribed shall be in the Fire.""**"
"7027","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, gave the same stern warning. > **""The briber and the bribed shall be in the Fire.""**"
"7028","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, specified bribery in judicial rulings. > **""Allah has cursed the briber and the bribed in judgment.""**"
"7029","ʿUlaym reports that they sat upon a roof with a Companion of the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him — ʿAbas al-Ghifari, may Allah be pleased with him — while people fled from the plague. ʿAbas thrice called upon the plague to take him. When questioned, since the Prophet forbade wishing for death, he cited the Prophet's command to hasten death before corruptions appear. > **""Hasten toward death before six: the rule of the foolish, the multitude of police, the sale of judgment, the belittling of blood, the severing of kinship, and a generation who take the Qurʾan as flutes, putting forward a man to chant for them though he is the least of them in understanding.""**"
"7030","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said he carried a report in his keeping which, if he disclosed, the people would stone him. He then prayed: O Allah, let me not reach the beginning of sixty. When asked what that meant, he listed the corruptions he feared: the rule of children, the sale of judgment, the multitude of police, testimony by acquaintance, treating trust as booty and charity as a fine, a generation who take the Qurʾan as flutes, and disregard for blood."
"7031","Masruq reports that he sat with ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man asked what unlawful devouring is. Ibn Masʿud replied that it is bribes in judgment, that this is disbelief, and then he recited: And whoever does not judge by what Allah has revealed — those are the disbelievers."
"7032","Ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that a bribe in judgment is disbelief, and among people it is unlawful devouring."
"7033","On his authority, may Allah be pleased with him, it is reported that he said unlawful devouring is bribery in religion."
"7034","Abu Humayd al-Saʿidi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, condemned gifts given to office-holders. > **""The gifts of officials are misappropriation.""**"
"7035","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warn the unqualified witness. > **""Whoever testifies against a Muslim a testimony he is not worthy to give, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"7036","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, contrasting the terror of the Day with the swift doom of the false witness. > **""Birds shall strike the earth with their beaks and shake their tails from the terror of the Day of Resurrection, yet the false witness shall barely speak, and his feet shall not leave the ground before he is flung into the Fire.""**"
"7037","Ibn ʿAbbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning testimony used to plunder and kill. > **""Whoever gives testimony by which the wealth of a Muslim is made lawful to seize and blood is shed has made the Fire obligatory for himself.""**"
"7038","Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning the one who withholds testimony when called. > **""Whoever conceals testimony when called to it is like one who testifies falsely.""**"
"7039","ʿAbdullah ibn Masʿud, may Allah be pleased with him, said that false testimony has been equated with associating partners with Allah, and he recited: And avoid false speech."
"7040","Abu al-Dardaʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, three grave warnings concerning obstruction of penalties, blind partisanship, and slander. > **""Whatever man whose intercession obstructs one of the fixed penalties of Allah shall remain in the wrath of Allah until he desists. Whatever man vents anger against a Muslim in a dispute he knows nothing about has opposed Allah in His right, sought His wrath, and upon him is the curse of Allah continuing until the Day of Resurrection. Whatever man spreads a word against a Muslim man, of which he is innocent, reviling him thereby in this world, it shall be binding upon Allah to melt him in the Fire on the Day of Resurrection until he produces fulfillment of what he said.""**"
"7041","In another narration from Abu al-Dardaʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, concerning defamation intended to disgrace. > **""Whoever mentions a man with something not in him in order to disgrace him thereby, Allah shall confine him in the Fire of Hell until he produces fulfillment of what he said concerning him.""**"
"7042","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, warned against five evils of dispute and falsehood. > **""Whoever's intercession obstructs one of the fixed penalties of Allah has opposed Allah in His dominion. Whoever aids in a dispute, not knowing whether it is truth or falsehood, remains in the wrath of Allah until he desists. Whoever walks with a people imagining himself a witness when he is no witness is like a false witness. Whoever claims a false dream shall be burdened with tying a knot between the two ends of a barley grain. Insulting the Muslim is wickedness, and fighting him is disbelief.""**"
"7043","Abu Salamah reports from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, distinguished this nation in inheritance and testimony. > **""One religious community does not inherit from another, nor is the testimony of one community valid against another — except my nation; their testimony is valid against all others.""**"
"7044","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was asked what witnesses suffice in a claim of nursing kinship. > **""One man or one woman""** — and in a narration — **""one man and one woman.""**"
"7045","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, accepted the testimony of the midwife."
"7046","ʿUmarah ibn Hazm, may Allah be pleased with him, testifies that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by an oath together with a single witness. Zayd ibn al-Hubab asked Malik ibn Anas whether this applies to divorce and emancipation; Malik replied that it does not, but concerns buying, selling, and the like."
"7047","Bilal ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by an oath together with a witness."
"7048","Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by an oath together with a witness."
"7049","Abu Saʿid al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by an oath together with a witness."
"7050","ʿAbdullah ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, judged by an oath together with a witness."
"7051","Jabir ibn ʿAbdullah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, attributed this judicial ruling to Gabriel's command. > **""Gabriel, peace be upon him, commanded me to judge by an oath together with a witness.""**"
"7052","Zaynab bint Thaʿlabah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sent his Companions who seized captives of Banu al-ʿAnbar though they had lived through both eras and had embraced Islam. Zabib, may Allah be pleased with him, hastened upon his she-camel and pleaded their case. Asked for proof, he offered the testimony of Samurah and his own oath, whereupon the Prophet ordered everything returned to Banu al-ʿAnbar. He then drew near to Zabib, wiped his hand from his head to his navel until Zabib felt the coolness of his palm, and prayed for him. > **""Have you proof, O Zabib? Return to Banu al-ʿAnbar everything belonging to them. O Allah, grant him pardon and well-being.""**"
"7053","ʿAdi ibn ʿAdi al-Kindi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that two men came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, disputing over land, each claiming it, one adding that he plowed and cultivated it. The Prophet administered the oath to the one in possession of the land."
"7054","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that two men disputed before the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, each bringing an equal number of upright witnesses. The Messenger drew lots between them and prayed. > **""O Allah, judge between them.""**"
"7055","Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that two men disputed before the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, over a camel, each establishing proof that it was his. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, divided it between them."
"7056","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, that he detained a man on suspicion pending inquiry."
"7057","In another narration, the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, required surety in a case of accusation."
"7058","Nubayshah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, detained a man on suspicion."
"7059","'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, may Allah have mercy on him, relates a compendium of the judgments of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: the mine is jubar, the well is jubar, and the wound inflicted by the dumb beast — grazing livestock or otherwise — is jubar, that is, a vain loss for which no compensation is exacted. He ﷺ decreed the fifth in rikaz (buried treasure); that the dates of pollinated palms belong to the one who pollinated them unless the buyer stipulates otherwise; that the property of a slave belongs to the seller unless the buyer stipulates otherwise; that the child belongs to the bed while the adulterer gets the stone; pre-emption among partners in lands and houses; the inheritance of Haml ibn Malik al-Hudhali from the wife slain by her co-wife; and for the slain foetus a ghurrah — a slave or slave-girl — to be inherited by her husband and sons. When the father of the condemned murderess protested in rhymed speech — how shall I pay for one who neither drank nor ate, neither cried nor raised his voice, for the like of that is void — the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared him to be of the soothsayers, on account of his rhymed affectation. He further relates judgments: seven cubits to be left for the road from an open courtyard upon it when its people wish to build therein — the road called al-Maqya; each of one to three disputed palms to have a precinct reaching to the extent of its branches; the upper palms to drink from the torrent before the lower, the water held to the ankles then released downward through the gardens in turn; a wife not to give from her wealth without her husband's permission; two grandmothers to share a sixth equally; one who frees his share in a slave to complete the manumission if he is solvent; no harm nor reciprocating harm; no right for a wrongful root; the surplus of a well in Madinah not to be withheld, nor surplus desert water withheld so as to withhold herbage; the major aggravated diyah as thirty bint labun, thirty hiqqah and forty khalifah, and the minor as thirty bint labun, thirty hiqqah, twenty bint makhad and twenty male ibn makhad — with 'Umar's, may Allah be pleased with him, later valuations in dirhams, additions for the sacred month and the sacred city completing twenty thousand for the two sanctuaries, taken from the Bedouins in livestock and from each people in the just value of their wealth. > **""The mine is jubar, the well is jubar, and the wound of the dumb beast is jubar. In rikaz there is the fifth. The fruit of the palms belongs to him who pollinated them unless the buyer stipulates, and the property of the slave belongs to him who sold him unless the buyer stipulates. The child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. This one is of the soothsayers. There shall be no harm nor reciprocating harm, and no right belongs to a wrongful root.""**"
"7060","Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn that one who stipulates a condition for his brother while intending never to fulfil it to him is like a man who lures his neighbour into an exposed place with no refuge. > **""Whoever stipulates a condition for his brother, not intending to fulfil it to him, is like one who lures his neighbour into a place with no defence.""**"
"7061","Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the ruling that Muslims stand by the conditions they stipulate, in matters that have been made lawful. > **""The Muslims are bound by their conditions in what has been made lawful.""**"
"7062","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports the nullification of every stipulation that finds no sanction in the Book of Allah, though such stipulations number a hundred. > **""Every condition not in the Book of Allah is void, even if there be a hundred conditions.""**"
"7063","'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports the grave warning that aiding an oppressor with falsehood in order to vanquish truth thereby casts one out of the covenant of Allah and the covenant of His Messenger ﷺ. > **""Whoever aids an oppressor with falsehood in order to defeat truth thereby has freed himself from the protection of Allah and the protection of His Messenger.""**"
"7064","Aws ibn Shurahbil of Banu Ashja', may Allah be pleased with him, heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that walking with an oppressor in order to aid him, while knowing him to be an oppressor, expels one from Islam. > **""Whoever walks with an oppressor to aid him, knowing that he is an oppressor, has departed from Islam.""**"
"7065","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports a cluster of warnings: that intercession standing between a hadd of Allah and its execution sets one in opposition to Allah in His dominion; that aiding a dispute while ignorant whether it is truth or falsehood keeps one under the wrath of Allah until he withdraws; that marching with a people as a supposed witness when one is no witness is false testimony; that feigning a dream burdens one with knotting the two ends of a grain of barley; and that reviling a Muslim is fisq while fighting him is kufr. > **""Whose intercession stands between a hadd of Allah and its execution has set himself against Allah in His dominion. Whoever aids a dispute, not knowing whether it is truth or falsehood, remains in the wrath of Allah until he withdraws. Whoever marches with a people deeming himself a witness when he is no witness is a false witness. Whoever feigns a dream shall be tasked with knotting the two ends of a grain of barley. Reviling a Muslim is fisq, and fighting him is kufr.""**"
"7066","'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, reports the Divine word that Allah's wrath burns severe against the one who wrongs a man who finds no helper but Him. > **""Allah says: My anger is severe against one who wrongs him who finds no helper other than Me.""**"
"7067","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports three traits of the character of the believers: anger that never draws into falsehood, contentment that never removes from truth, and power that never grasps what is not one's own. > **""Three are of the character of the believers: when one is angered, his anger does not draw him into falsehood; when he is pleased, his pleasure does not remove him from truth; and when he is empowered, he does not grasp what is not his.""**"
"7068","'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ had a covenant written between the Muhajirun and the Ansar: that they jointly bear their blood-money obligations, ransom their captive with kindness, and maintain reconciliation among the Muslims. > **""That they jointly bear their blood-money obligations, ransom their absent one with kindness, and maintain reconciliation among the Muslims.""**"
"7069","Mukhawwal al-Bahdhi, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he had set snares at al-Abwa' in which a gazelle was caught, then escaped and was seized by another man. Both disputants came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, neither yielding to his fellow, so he ﷺ divided it between them."
"7070","'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib, brother of 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, reports that al-'Abbas had a spout over the road of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them. On a Friday, as 'Umar passed in his Friday garments, water mixed with the blood of two chicks slaughtered for al-'Abbas poured from the spout upon him, so 'Umar ordered the spout torn out, then returned, cast off his garments, put on others, and led the people in prayer. Al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him swearing by Allah that the Prophet ﷺ himself had set it in that very place, whereupon 'Umar adjured him to climb upon his own back and restore it to the exact spot where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had placed it — and al-'Abbas did so."
"7071","Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that after the passing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, al-'Abbas disputed with 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, over things the Prophet ﷺ had left behind. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, refused to disturb what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had left undisturbed, and 'Umar after him took the same stand. When the dispute was brought before 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, he fell silent and bowed his head. Fearing he might seize it, Ibn 'Abbas struck his hand between the shoulders of al-'Abbas, his father, and adjured him to surrender it to 'Ali — and he surrendered it to him."
"7072","A venerable elder of Quraysh of Banu Taym, transmitting from six or seven Qurashis including 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them, reports that 'Ali and al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, entered upon 'Umar raising their voices over what had been in the hands of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. 'Umar bade al-'Abbas desist — knowing his plea that as the nephew he owned half the estate — and 'Ali — knowing his plea through the Prophet's daughter under him — reminding them how the Prophet ﷺ had disposed of it, and how Abu Bakr after him and 'Umar himself had striven to follow that practice. 'Umar swore that Abu Bakr had truthfully heard the Prophet ﷺ say that the Prophet is not inherited, his legacy being for the poor and needy of the Muslims, and that the Prophet does not die until one of his ummah has led him in prayer. He offered to hand the estate to the two disputants only if they would administer it by the practice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and of Abu Bakr — so they withdrew, returned, and al-'Abbas willingly ceded it to 'Ali. > **""The Prophet is not inherited; his legacy is for the poor and needy of the Muslims. The Prophet does not die until one of his ummah has led him in prayer.""**"
"7073","Ibn Sirin reports that al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, declared that from Jabars to Jabaliq — the east to the west, as Ma'mar glosses — no man would be found whose grandfather was a Prophet but he and his brother. He counselled gathering upon Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, adding that he knew not but that it might be a trial for them and enjoyment for a time."
"7074","Al-Sha'bi reports that he witnessed al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, at al-Nukhaylah when Mu'awiyah made peace with him. Mu'awiyah bade him rise, address the people, and announce his surrender of authority — in one transmission, to inform them of the affair he had abandoned. Al-Hasan rose upon the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then declared that the wisest wisdom is piety and the most foolish folly is immorality; that the authority disputed between him and Mu'awiyah — if it had been his own right — he surrendered to Mu'awiyah seeking the rectitude of this ummah and the sparing of its blood, and if it had been the right of one more entitled to it than he, he likewise yielded it; adding that he knew not but that it might be a trial for them and enjoyment for a time."
"7075","Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that when the day came for 'Ali and Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with them, to meet at Dumat al-Jandal, Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, told him it ill became him — the in-law of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the son of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab — to lag behind a peace through which Allah would reconcile the ummah of Muhammad ﷺ. Mu'awiyah arrived that day upon a great Bactrian camel asking who aspired to authority and craned his neck toward it. Ibn 'Umar, who had never before entertained worldly ambition, was moved to answer that it is aspired to by him who fought him and his father over Islam until he brought them into it — but he remembered Paradise and its bliss, and turned away."
"7076","Suhayb, freedman of al-'Abbas, reports that al-'Abbas sent him to summon 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, whom he found feeding the people; 'Uthman finished feeding them and came, greeting Abu al-Fadl with a prayer for a prosperous face. Al-'Abbas adjured him by Allah concerning 'Ali — his cousin, his brother in religion, his companion with the Prophet ﷺ, and his in-law — having heard that 'Uthman meant to move against 'Ali and his companions, and asked to be excused from any part in it. 'Uthman answered that he first granted the intercession for 'Ali: had 'Ali wished, none would have stood before him, unless he refused, in which case the matter rested on his own stands. He then sent to 'Ali adjuring him by ties of cousinhood, brotherhood in religion, companionship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the pledge of allegiance — and 'Ali swore by Allah that were he commanded to go forth from his very house, he would go forth."
"7077","Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her in the year of the Conquest. She asked him to shield her from 'Ali; when he asked what was the matter, she said a man had come to her seeking her protection, but 'Ali told her to stand aside from him lest he pierce her with his spear — and indeed pricked the front of her head. The Prophet ﷺ reassured her that 'Ali would never have stabbed her. > **""'Ali would never have stabbed you.""**"
